A||||57||theogre||Brake Upgrade Math||||6||
Z||000000||theogre||01-10-2001||07:47 PM||||I hate it when I don't know the numbers behind certain things. The Grand Am/Beretta brake upgrade is one of those things. I ran the numbers on the front hydraulics today. The results were interesting. (It took forever today to find all those bloody numbers.....)<P>As most people here have read, you can do this with the OE master cyl but you get really long pedal travel. To fix that the MC from a full size 90 blazer is used instead.<P>The ratio of piston areas for the 2 setups is very close to the same. The Blazer MC & Grand Am caliper has a very slight increase in ratio. You can, for now, get the excel sheet I did the math on here. <A HREF="http://theogre.home.sprynet.com/brake_math.xls" TARGET=_blank>Download/view brake_math.xls</A> It will probably be integrated into a cave article soon so this link may only be good for a limited time.<P>I did not include the rear caliper yet. I can't find the bore spec. Does anyone have an acurate bore/piston size for the stock rear? I can measure it but even a 1mm error has a big effect on the math. all I have is a ruler. Could someone with access to a micrometer measure the bore and/or piston. (The piston can be measured without removing it from the caliper. push the seal back so you can get on the piston not just the lip that holds the pad clip.)||||reg||6
Z||000001||william_thorniii||01-10-2001||08:17 PM||||theogre,<P>You always have to do things the hard way.  I could have provided all of those pesky calculations for you.  <P>I have the rear piston bore at home in my MVMA manual.  I just have to remember to dig it up or bring it in to work tomorrow.  I am offline at home.  <P>William T. Thorn, III<P>||||reg||3
Z||000002||bHooper||01-10-2001||09:57 PM||||ogre, nice work.  just one question... what does it mean?  are you saying mathmatically it doesn't immprove performance?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"><P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||01-10-2001||11:35 PM||||Bill, Thanks, I measured the thing with a ruler but I'd still like the official number. The math itself wasn't hard. gave me something to do with Excel which I hardly ever use. I would apreciate it if you double check the math if you have time.<P>BH, It depends on just how you do the upgrade. I'll be adding the rear caliper info tomorow after Bill double checks the spec for it. The important columns are the last 2.<P>On the front... for each 10 pounds you put into the MC you get 2.5 pounds more at the wheel with the blazer/grand am than with the OE set. (For each 10# in OE gives 37.2# vs B/G 39.7#)<P>The alarming numbers are the rear. You'll see why when I post the rest of it. If you put in a blazer MC with the OE rear brakes you lose significant braking on that axle. If you put the GA calipers back there you gain a bunch.<P>Either way I no longer believe this upgrade can be done safely without reproportioning the system. I think a number of the ones aready done without that have problems that could rear their heads at very bad times. Quite frankly, the rear numbers scared me.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Uaana||01-11-2001||02:35 AM||||So are you saying that doing the B/G upgrade on the front putting in a Blazer MC and leaving the rear stock is the way to go.<BR>Math is nice.<BR>But I work better with english.||||reg||1
Z||000005||MrPBody||01-11-2001||08:12 AM||||So, with Grand Am brakes and a Blazer master cylinder, the improvement over stock brakes is due to larger pad area, greater effective rotor diameter, and rotor venting?  (Not greater hydraulic area ratio.)<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Carrolles||01-11-2001||09:37 AM||||Uaana,<P>I believe that Theogre said that the Blazer master cylinder with the grand am front brake upgrade and oem rear brakes results in poor rear brakes.  The balance between front and rear brake load is too heavily biased to the front.  This feels great under normal braking situations but could get very ugly in panic situations or poor road conditions.<P>I've thought about doing the grand am upgrade but I don't like this bias problem and I don't want to lose my e-brake (I have a 4 spd and need the parking brake) by doing the grand am rear upgrade.  I'm not ready to spend the bucks to do a real engineered upgrade yet and I don't feel comfortable with an adjustable proportioning valve.||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||01-11-2001||07:13 PM||||Carroll that's correct. Putting the GA front and blazer MC screws up the rear brakes. The lose in rear braking is quite large.<P>VERY LONG POST ALERT!<P>Well, I spent a bunch of time working on this…(We'll ignore people doing this as a cost reduction project. BTW, If you think cost of Fiero parts is bad, price the brake parts for Trans Am and some of the other all disk cars. Fiero is cheap compared to many.)<P>The Blazer/Grand Am upgrade gives us 2 main things. <BR>Increased clamping force in the caliper.<BR>Improved fade resistance.<BR>I don't think there is enough difference in rotor working surface or pad contact to account for much change there. (Keep in mind. the GA calipers and pads fit the OE brackets…)<P>That said… <P>I doubt anyone here, excluding racers, has ever experienced brake fade in Fiero. I've driven the hell out of my car and never gotten a case of fade. Current Semi Metallic pads are very difficult to get that hot. Unless your brakes are messed up, or you can't seem to keep you foot off the pedal, you are very unlikely to fade in most street driving conditions.<P>It seems everyone has missed the fact improved Clamping force can be attained two ways. You can make the caliper pistons bigger like the B/G upgrade <B>OR</B> you can decrease the size of the MC.<P>Reducing the MC even fractions of a mm increases clamping <B>but</B> it does this about evenly to both axles. You'll have a slight increase in pedal travel but it won't be like the difference between the Fiero and Blazer MC on the GA calipers. (Just for ref, the Blazer MC is over 3 mm larger than Fiero OE.)<P>If you drop the MC diameter 1.4mm (90 S10 blazer 24mm MC) the ratio on the front caliper goes from 3.722 to 4.168, rear goes from 3.139 to 3.515. In Front, that gives us an additional 4.46 pounds of force at the piston face for every 10 on the MC piston. ((If I have my hydraulics right…. Because the caliper is floating, we end up with 8.92 additional pounds of clamping.)) We actually get a bigger increase in caliper pressure than the B/G upgrade. Yet because it effects front and rear similarly we stay allot closer to the OE balance.<P>Will, the S10 Blazer MC fit? Heck if I know but I intend to find out. GM uses pretty much the same MC layout for everything so I don't see why not. Probably just needs the tank changed.<P>I'm also looking for other MC's that are even closer in size to the OE one. I’d like to find one that is less than 1mm smaller. <P>If you look at the new version of the spreadsheet, you can see the math I was messing with today as well.<P>As for Fiero Store's kit… From all the evidence I can gather it is just a prefab based on the same flawed data all over the web. From what I can tell so far they have also failed to address the machining issues brought up in another thread. The info they post about the bigger MC is wrong. Putting a larger MC on does not increase caliper force. It reduces it. ((I think Fiero Store need to consult their lawyer and insurance agent very carefully over the brake upgrade products they offer.))<P>When you really dig into it, this whole “upgrade” comes off looking like someone jammed on a bunch of parts thru trial and error and shoved in a replacement proportioning valve as an after thought. The B/G upgrade has some really major flaws.<P>Is Fiero Braking bad? That depends on who you ask. If you believe the writers of magazines with nothing to do but fart around driving cars and making half assed at best statements about them….. Or if you actually sit down and compare Fiero brake performance to other cars. If you look at specs for other cars built in the same time period Fiero is as good or better than many. The only time I can think that you might need to change the braking is if you have a big motor or you are racing the car. <BR>||||reg||4
Z||000008||MrPBody||01-11-2001||08:54 PM||||<I>Very interesting</I> . . .<P>I can say that my '85 SE seems to have very little reserve stopping power.  It stops OK in normal use, but in an all-out bend-the-pedal panic stop, I can barely lock the front wheels on dry pavement.  But maybe my pads are toast.<P>I'm skittish of the Grand Am swap because of unsprung weight and wheel set-out.  (When I have deliberately offset them inward!)  Your analysis shows other reasons to worry.<P>May we assume that higher line pressure with a smaller m/cyl is no problem?<P>As an alternative, is there any prospect of using a larger booster?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||fiero56||01-11-2001||09:18 PM||||Ogre, posted on behalf of WTIII:<P>48mm rear piston diameter<BR>1.88" for same<P>||||reg||1
Z||000010||FieroJoe||01-11-2001||09:18 PM||||I just redid everything on my brake system, except replacing the MC.  All new calipers, rotors, pads.  I have never had a problem with them fading dangerously, and I just have the lifetime warranty pads that get replaced when they are worn for free.  I know I can lock up the front two, and once I think I locked up all 4.||||reg||1
Z||000011||daved||01-11-2001||09:45 PM||||Without messing with the hydraulics, the only other way to increase braking force is to increase the diameter of the rotor...and move the caliper out to suit.  The force increases with the square of the distance.  Just look at those giant brake rotors on race cars, but the calipers are not significantly larger. <BR>Anyone thought how this could be done on our cars?||||reg||1
Z||000012||lawrence||01-11-2001||10:57 PM||||theogre,<P>I hear what you're saying about mixing/matching parts.  That's the very reason I went with larger rotors, while keeping everything else the same.  I didn't want the hassles of trying to get the pedal travel and pressures matched correctly.  Using the stock m/c and calipers meant that all the movements of parts would stay the same.  Yes, I'm stuck with the expensive '88 calipers, but that's OK with me.<P>As far as the rears are concerned, I still say that there is not enough pressure going back there.  If you read my article, I mention that I removed the pressure spring from the proportioning valve.  This is the opposite of what you are calculating using the Blazer m/c.  If you read Randy's article, he states that just doing the front caliper upgrade gave him "too much pinch" in the fronts.  Also, I remember when Paul Vargyas first did the G/A upgrade to the rears of his car, he noticed a big difference in stopping power.  By increasing the caliper bore in the rear, he increased the braking power in the rear.  Since he noticed the difference, that tells me that there isn't enough being done back there on a stock system.<P>-L<BR> <A HREF="http://www.webelectricmagazine.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.webelectricmagazine.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||rodmcneill||01-12-2001||12:40 AM||||I remember reading somewhere that the 88 Fiero's had smaller diameter master cyls. Would'nt it be great if brake performance could be upgraded with just a master cyl. replacement. I was thinking about doing the G.A. brake (upgrade?) change, after reading recent different posts I'm glad I did'nt. Now if we could just find out the coefficent of friction of different brands of brake pads...||||reg||1
Z||000014||lowCG||01-12-2001||01:04 AM||||Stock Fiero brakes are scary.<BR>My big brake project may happen soon if I can keep from driving the car long enough to tear it apart,10.5" rotors,and 4 piston BMW calipers,with a Blazer diesel M/C.The piston areas are near the original ratios.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Uaana||01-12-2001||03:40 AM||||Ok now I'm a little torn on this issue.<BR>When I still had my 4 cyl 85 I could stop on a dime with the ridges to spare. But when I got my GT brakes needed to be replaced so I went with the front rotor/caliper exchange and left everything else stock.<BR>Now I get ok braking but not the even feel that I had with the stock system in the SE.<BR>I plan on swapping everything back to stock as soon as i finish my current project.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Carrolles||01-12-2001||08:51 AM||||Lawrence,<P>When you installed the larger rotors did you move the caliper farther out form the center of the axel to take advantage of the bigger diameter?  If not I don't see how larger rotors helps.<P>I remember an article in FOCOA magazine about Fiero brakes that showed a larger rotor setup.  It included brakets to move the caliper out closer to the edge of the rotor.<P>Take care,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000017||lawrence||01-12-2001||12:32 PM||||Yes, I made brackets to mount the stock calipers out about 3/4" on the radius.<P>-L<BR> <A HREF="http://www.webelectricmagazine.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.webelectricmagazine.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000018||Will||01-12-2001||02:11 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by lawrence:<BR><B>Yes, I made brackets to mount the stock calipers out about 3/4" on the radius.<P>-L<BR> <A HREF="http://www.webelectricmagazine.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.webelectricmagazine.com</A> </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Did you use OEM rotors from another car or did you buy aftermarket rotors?||||reg||
Z||000019||lawrence||01-12-2001||05:51 PM||||I used rear rotors from a 97 Firebird.  And they're cheap, too.  ~$20 each.  You can see a picture or two in this article:<BR> <BR> <A HREF="http://www.webelectricmagazine.com/99/4/brakes.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.webelectricmagazine.com/99/4/brakes.htm</A> <P>Good luck,<P>-L<BR> <A HREF="http://www.webelectricmagazine.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.webelectricmagazine.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000020||rockcrawl||01-12-2001||06:20 PM||||Ogre, I don't have software to view your spreadsheet, so I just wanted to make sure you are aware that all of the master cylinders you mentioned have different size bores for front and rear hydraulic circuits. The S-10 MC is smaller in the front circuit only, the rear is the same (36mm) meaning it would provide even more front bias. A 90 blazer diesel has a 30.0 mm bore for front & rear, which it seems will help balance things out F/R, but 30mm might be too large or too small. <P>rodmcneill,<BR> The 88 has a 24mm F & 36mm R master cyl (same size as 90 S-10), the 87 has 25.4mm F & 36mm R.<P><P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000021||theogre||01-12-2001||07:22 PM||||Lawrence, It's been awhile so I reread your brake article. It's the only other one I've seen that actually explains anything behind the upgrade in detail. Problem is it also requires bigger wheels to do it. The majority only has 13-14" wheels. I also agree the OE prop valve is set lame. My calculations do not include the effect of the prop valve. I like the way you resprung the valve. Once it's set no one can F it up like an adjustable one. (Do you still have the ordering info for the springs you tried?)<P>Everyone, For a small change in MC size I don't think there should be a problem. It's the equivalent effect of having the GA calipers with the OE MC. ((If the rubber line sections look bad replace them before doing any playing. You should replace them anyway but you definitely don't want to play and have iffy lines.))<P>So far the slightly smaller MC is the least problematic upgrade I can think of. It gives a little more pressure to both front and rear. It keeps the ratios closer to OE. You may still want or need to tweak the proportioning valve but it won't be the giant changes needed to make the GA parts balance right. If you don't like the results, it's easy and cheap to undo the changes. If all you want to do is play with this, you could get a used MC from a scrap yard for cheap. Check prices though, sometimes used is close to the price of the rebuilt ones. (I'll likely try this but not until spring.)<P>I'm still digging in parts books over the next few days. I'd like to find an MC that is between 24 and 25mm diameter. I want to have several alternatives in small increments to play with. So far I've found 24mm is the closest. I'd like at least one closer if possible. (There are a bunch of GM cars that have the 24 mm one. Oddly this includes Grand Am)<P>I did check 88. The MC specs I see are still 25.4mm. The same as all the older ones.<P>WT, 48mm for the rear piston? Interesting. I have one I tore off an 85. I get 45mm. I was really careful about the measurement.<P>DaveD, You're correct, and that's what Lawrence did. It won't work on the 13-14 inch wheels most of us have. That's the advantage of the Grand Am parts, they bolt to existing brackets and use the same rotor size. (or really close to it.) The FSBlazer & Grand Am parts have their own set of problems.<P>Rockcrawl, I know some are “step bore” and some aren’t The limited access I have to parts databases makes it hard to tell which is which. My main point is that a slightly smaller MC gives us a similar effect. Do you know of one that is 24mm straight bore? Is Fiero OE Step Bore? I don’t have one to take apart and look at. In some books it looks like it might be other’s only list it at 25.4mm. I’m going to try to check out the Grand Am, which oddly is listed as 24mm and a couple others at the scrap yard this weekend.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000022||lawrence||01-13-2001||11:52 AM||||You are correct.  I was able to install larger rotors because I have the 15" wheels.  I actually lost some diameter up front due to the thinner 88 wheels.  However, at least you don't have to go to non-stock 16" or larger wheels.<P>Another thing to consider is the shape/size of the non-88 calipers.  Just looking at pictures, it looks like the 88 calipers are smaller than the earlier ones.  This may be why I can stuff them under the 15" wheels, while all the other large-rotor upgrades require bigger wheels.<P>Either way, there might be some space between the caliper and wheel, regardless of the diameter of the wheel.  Obviously, the 13" wheels will be closer, so it may be more work than it's worth, but converting to stock 15" wheels should be much cheaper than going to after market 16" or larger wheels.<P>As for the prop valve spring, I removed it and have nothing in there now.  With this setup, the fronts lock first, then the rears, and sometimes the rears still don't lock.  This is on dry pavement, of course, and I try not to do that often B^)<P>There's still the issue of the power booster that I've tried to explain.  The booster will only give you so much boost.  That's why the larger bore m/c doesn't work.  If your leg had infinite push power you wouldn't need a booster, and you could use any m/c that you wanted, as long as you had enough stroke inside the car (pedal didn't it the floor).  If you could put on a larger diameter, or tandem booster, then you'd have enough boost, and again, you could put on a larger diameter m/c.<P>Your leg is strong enough to push beyond what the booster can do.  This is what happens when you install a larger m/c.  You think the pedal is hitting the floor, but it's really just going 'through' the booster, and it goes solid.  If you remove the vacuum line from the booster, you will think that the pedal is hitting solid steel.  But it isn't, it's just pushing on the hydraulics (try it, it's amazing, but don't drive down the street!).<P>If I had lots of time (and a younger back) I would put a smaller m/c in to increase the pressure at the calipers.  Then, I would remount the pedal pivot points to reduce the stroke.  My leg is strong enough to push the driver's side of the booster hard enough to overcome the pedal disadvantage.  It just isn't strong enough to push the m/c directly.<P>-L<BR> <A HREF="http://www.webelectricmagazine.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.webelectricmagazine.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000023||Monkeyman||01-14-2001||12:52 AM||||I have absolutely no idea what you guys are talking about, but let me give you my seat-of-the-pants commentary on the G/A brakes/Blazer MC upgrade.  It's not worth the money/time spent.  I upgraded (not the correct word) the very worn out brakes on the Grape Ape to the Fiero Stores front kit and Autozones parts for the rear.  I got Earls SS braided brake lines (looked nice!) and a Blazer MC.  I definitely saw an improvement in braking (once I got some of the bugs worked out), but I would imagine the same improvement could have been realized with new stock brake components.  I don't have the time nor the expertise to do a fancy, home made braking system so my only alternative to stock (gotta keep the rear calipers stock for the ebrake now that I have a stick) is the G/A deal.  I'll replace the pads and have the rotors turned on my new GT, but there's no way in heck I'll spend another $350 on alternate parts.<P>I'll get off my soapbox now. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000024||Standard||01-14-2001||09:02 AM||||I was able to lock the my 195/60/15's on dry pavement (complete with a nice little cloud of smoke) with my stock kinda mushy brakes..  if I ever get around to replacing the bleeder screw on the f & r drivers side calipers and fully bleed the system, I think I'll have more than enough braking power.  I have new pads all around, and Earl's SS lines on the passenger side, 'cause those bleeders weren't frozen to the calipers...||||reg||
Z||000025||rockcrawl||01-14-2001||09:03 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Rockcrawl, I know some are “step bore” and some aren’t The limited access I have to parts databases makes it hard to tell which is which. My main point is that a slightly smaller MC gives us a similar effect. Do you know of one that is 24mm straight bore? Is Fiero OE Step Bore? I don’t have one to take apart and look at. In some books it looks like it might be other’s only list it at 25.4mm. I’m going to try to check out the Grand Am, which oddly is listed as 24mm and a couple others at the scrap yard this weekend.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I'm using the Raybestos RCD-3 electronic brake parts catalog for info. 90 f/s Blazer 1-1/8"&1-37/64", 90 S-10 24&36 w/power 31.75&24 w/o power, 84-87 Fiero 25.4&36, 88 Fiero 24&36, 87 Grand Am 24mm, 90 Grand Am 7/8", 85 G/A 7/8"&1-1/4". I haven't checked which ones will fit the fiero. It looks like they are all step-bore except the 87 & 90 Grand Am, and the previously mentioned diesel truck.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000026||theogre||01-14-2001||12:14 PM||||Jon, doesn't that just crap on my tables. I'll rerun the whole mess next week. It'll give me something to do between calls. Physically most of the ones I've seen that are on a power booster look like they will fit with minimum trouble.<P>Lawrence, The older calipers use a pad that is shaped to the rotor. It is radiused on both inside and outside diameters. The outside pad is around 4.5 inches long. The 88 pad looks a little less picky about the rotor as long as the pad fits on the working surface. I'm not sure I follow what you're getting at with the booster. As long as we don't grosely increase pedal travel it should be a non issue.<P>Wait a minute.... Does anyone have more detail on the step bore MC and how it works? I'm looking at the pictures I have and the placement of things doesn't add up. (I haven't rebuilt an MC in years.) It looks as if the large bore may be some type of failover. I'm going to have to get a scraped one of these and see just how it works. I don't trust the pictures in Haynes. If we go by those pictures then the primary seal of both pistons is in the small bore. That means we effectivly have only the small bore doing the braking. In that case the table I did doesn't change. (If you look at the location of the rear outlet on the MC and how long the piston is there is no way it should be using the larger bore in normal braking.)<P>do we have good email adresses for raybestos or bendix, I have one for ACdelco. I'll try them. We have to determine exactly when the second bore size is relevant. It would be extremely odd that a main braking bore has only one seal between it and the atmosphere. ||||reg||1
Z||000027||batboy||01-14-2001||02:00 PM||||I'm glad I held off getting a M/C for my Grand Am brake swap. Is the change to G/A calipers and rotors an ideal brake upgrade? Well, yes and no. For serious performance driving and SCCA racing...probably not. For a MODEST increase in braking on a street driven car at a budget price, then yes. Of course the lack of rear e-brake comes back to haunt us.<P>I did the front G/A brakes first and tried them too. Way to much braking on the front. I then did the rear brake swap too. Much better. I think even in stock form, the back brakes need a little more squeeze than they're normally getting. Probably the G/A swap needs more rear braking too (don't overdo it) I really think using a high quality adjustable proportioning would work just fine. Street rodders have been using them for years.||||reg||1
Z||000028||theogre||01-14-2001||03:56 PM||||I sent this to ACDelco....<P>Hi, Some friends and I are little confused about “step bore” master cylinders used on many current GM products. When we look at the diagrams for the thing it looks like all the braking is done by the smaller size bore. All the pictures and diagrams we can find have the primary seal on the rear piston in the smaller bore along with the front piston. Is the larger bore just a failover safety thing? We can’t find much information to go with the pictures. (The pictures are more about how to rebuild one than how the thing works.) Is there someplace we can find a better explanation of these? Thanks, <P>It usually takes them a couple days to respond. monday being a holiday it may take longer.||||reg||1
Z||000029||FIEROCIOUS||01-14-2001||04:29 PM||||As some of you may know I race high speed SCCA style races in Nevada. My observations are as follows<P>Test conditions:<BR>10 laps peak speed of 100 mph. 100 to 20 stoping speeds. Maximum brakeing without lock up.<BR>Stock brakes: All new PG pads, new rotors and high temp dot 4 fluid.<P>Stock brake feel: Second lap VERY mushy pore modulation basicly push and pray. No possibility for lock up after lap two. Brake feel remained nearly same from lap two on.<P>Stock brake temp: Tested imediately after 10 laps 950 *f both front and rear or realy F'n hot! (temps within 20 *f frnt to rr) <P>G/A upgrade, stainless lines, Blazer M/C and brute stop pads and high temp dot 4 fluid.<P>G/A brake feel: More pedal effort while cold, progressive fade through out laps fair modulation even on lap 10. Fair pedal pressure required but plenty left for lock up on lap 10. Rears become more prevelent through out laps.<P>G/A brake temp: Taken imediately after 10 laps 620 front 570 Rear (temps within 50 *f Frnt to rr) <P>Evaluation of brakes G/A produced significant reduction in lap times 59 seconds stock vrs. 56 G/A  Stock brakes produced more even brakeing but faded quickly. <P>solution: Higher friction rear brake pads for G/A brakes. I may use the raysbestos PG's front and Brute stop on the rears.<P>For what ever it's worth?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000030||theogre||01-14-2001||04:35 PM||||FIEROCIOUS, The work I'm trying to do here is mostly related to braking in daily driving but that is still useful. Thanks. It does confirm the GA rotors may be worth the trouble to those with heavy braking like autocross.||||reg||1
Z||000031||theogre||01-16-2001||08:51 PM||||Well.... ACDelco Can't/Won't answer the question. I'll have to try Wagner and the others.....||||reg||1
Z||000032||JasonC||01-27-2001||01:46 PM||||Thanks for all the info here. Keep this post alive. I want a good set of breaks in my 84SE V8 but I don't race it. From all the notes here looks like just staying with what I have is best/safest, and just put new oem on.<P>------------------<BR>Jason Carmichael<BR> <IMG SRC="http://cwm.ragesofsanity.com/s/net2/smile-r.gif"> <A HREF="http://www.getpaid2surf.net/97GPGT" TARGET=_blank>My GP GT on the net.</A> <IMG SRC="http://cwm.ragesofsanity.com/s/net2/smile-l.gif"> ||||reg||7
Z||000033||theogre||01-27-2001||02:06 PM||||I am still working on this. I'm going to have to get a scrap MC to disect and see just how the 36mm bore is used. I may have to get a couple and have one split long way with a band saw so I can see how it's guts line up. It's going to be awhile before I can do that.<P>I started all this because I was going to do this grand am thing to my own car. The more I got into it the more questions were raised without any good answer.<P>I'm no longer planing to change my own car but I am still working on this because of the number of people with this "upgrade" and those considering doing it.<P>I'm worried about this enough that if I was Fiero Store or anyone else offering this for retail sale I'd be calling my lawyers and liablilty insurance people.||||reg||1
Z||000034||rockcrawl||01-27-2001||11:29 PM||||Ogre,<BR> Please accept my apology, I think I have steered you in the wrong direction. I remember learning about these master cylinders somewhere along the line. I believe the large bore is for rapid displacement of fluid, to move the pistons quickly into position. Then the small bore takes over and supplies the needed pressure. That way you get the short throw of a large bore and the pressure of a small bore all in the same unit. Does this make any sense? Sorry if I misled you and delayed your research. Even if this is how they work, it still should be a consideration when swapping parts, and worth looking into.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||11
Z||000035||theogre||01-28-2001||10:58 AM||||Rock, So far that is the only answer I've seen that makes any sense... It also would account for why all the metric step bores above have a 36mm secondary bore.<P>If what you are saying is accurate, and I suspect it is, then the math as I have it in the table is accurate.<P>I think we can try a smaller MC without much trouble. I'll likely grab one from the scrap yard as soon as weather is warmer. I need to do a bunch of work anyway that will mean bleeding. If I don't like the smaller MC then it's no big deal to put it back to OE. ||||reg||1
Z||000036||87GTZ34||01-28-2001||11:31 PM||||Ogre - Don't wait for warmer weather to go hit the yards... Although its cold this year in Fl, its bearable (73 degrees  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">).  This is an excellent thread so I'll volunteer to go on a yard expedition just point me in the direction of what you want.  Crawler (aka John) was correct in the 2 stage operation for fluid displacement (GM calls it the 'quick take up feature', someone got a patent...).  I have the '87 GM manual and that is in the gen desc for the MC.  So in theory, you want the largest take up piston and a smaller than stock pressure piston.||||reg||1
Z||000037||theogre||02-19-2001||10:07 PM||||87GTZ34, I missed your post.... sorry.<P>There's more going on than the weather I need to worry about right now. I'd have to find a suitable MC and install the thing and test it. It's not a 10 minute job to do it right. For obvious reasons the testing has to be done very carefully and will take several days at least.<P>All the MC's we are worried about have the 36mm "takeup" bore. I'm looking for the main bore to be 24-25mm. There are a couple farther up the thread that are candidates. The best thing to do would be take an old Fiero MC to the yard so you can verify that the mounting points, flair nuts, etc all fit. It also has to properly fit the MC piston on the booster pin. It probably easier to do than explain.<P>My other concern is the legal end of it. I don't want to release an illegal, or worse dangerous, item into the community like the Grand Am upgrade. The more I've found out about that one the less I like it. I'll be releasing a more complete statement about that soon.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 02-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000038||HarryG||02-20-2001||12:04 PM||||Anyone else read the article on brakes in the Feb. 2001 issue of GrassRoots Motorsports?<P>||||reg||3
Z||000039||MontanaFiero||02-20-2001||03:02 PM||||Here is the article HarryG was talking about: <A HREF="http://www.grassrootsmotorsports.com/brakes.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.grassrootsmotorsports.com/brakes.html</A> <P>Now, I have a question... Keeping that article in mind, and theogre's mastercylinder idea in mind... How much brake performance would you gain using carbon-metallic pads and standard sized  cross-drilled rotors.  What are your feelings/ experiences with cross-drilled rotors?<P>------------------<BR>&gt;&gt; '87 Coupe 4 cyl, auto &lt;&lt;<BR>&gt;&gt; '86 SE V6, 4 speed- <I>project</I> &lt;&lt;||||reg||1
Z||000040||VISCERAL||02-20-2001||03:22 PM||||I have done the upgrade all the way around and I know I do not need a blazer m/c. I have had this setup for the last year now. I used Performance Friction Carbon Mettalic pads and they are the best pads I have ever owned. Brake pedal height is actually higher than stock and the braking is great. I will move up to 11.25" brakes this summer though. What can I say, I just like the fact that they are there in case I need them.<P>------------------<BR>Patrick Szewczyk<BR>1987 Fiero GT<BR>Silver, 5 spd, sunroof centerforce dualfriction clutch, Koni's all around, Ground Control coilovers in the rear, lowered 2" - susp.tech springs-front, poly bushings: control arms-sway bars-cradle-motor-dogbone, rear sway bar, pioneer cd player, 180 stat, 176-166 fan switch, MSD Coil, Rodney Dickman's short shifter, bushings, etc..., grand am brakes all around, k&n filter, Mustang rear scoop, next year V-8 + lowered spindles + 17x8 and 17x9" Mas Italy wheels +  235/45/17 and 255/40/17's + new paint+ custom hood vents + Mr. Mikes leather interior + 11.25" brakes w/ parking brake.||||reg||1
Z||000041||HarryG||02-20-2001||03:29 PM||||MontanaFiero:<BR>I hadn't read the linked article you referenced: the one I mentioned was in the hard copy mag. Won't have the author's name till tomorrow.<BR>Harry ||||reg||1
Z||000042||theogre||02-20-2001||07:09 PM||||Montana, cross drilling is a waste managment tool. not a cooling tool. What if any improvment there is is tied directly to the pads used with them. If the pads are a already low waste or waste controled thru their manufacturing then drilled/slotted rotors are unlikely to do anything. Do a search we've been over and over that topic.<P>I don't have the article referenced in print. I looked at the linked one breifly. I'll look more later. The linked article is targeted at race applications. Be carefull how you translate that information to the street. <P>The initial premis, that choice of friction material is critical. translates to all aplications. It's probably a large part of why many people think Fiero brakes are weak. Application matching brake friction to a car is hard enough. matching to the driver is even harder.<P>Others,<P>Keep in mind the issues here are for cars driven on the street. <P>This is the last time I'll say this in this thread. There are legal issues tied to all street driven changes that don't apply to race only cars. You can easily find yourself several million $ in depbt because of using the grand am "upgrade" package on a street driven car.<P>The rear axle version of the Grand Am/Berreta upgrade is ILLEGAL in the U.S. due to the elimination of the hand brake system. Your insurance company can and likely will refuse to pay out on any car with illegally modified brakes involved in an accident.<P>These kinds of issues are why I don't just slap another MC on the car and go for a ride. It is the entire reason the linked Excel sheet I did exists. I won't even try to do this untill I have at least a 3 day weekend. Not because the work take that long. Changing an MC is less than an hour unless a bleeder screw is froze. It will take that much time to test it before I'd do so much as drive to work durring rush hour.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 02-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000043||MrPBody||02-20-2001||09:44 PM||||Based on the linked Grassroots Motorsports article -- and advice from Carbotech -- I installed Carbotech Super Street pads on my '85 SE.  My car stops a hell of a lot better now.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000044||HarryG||02-21-2001||12:12 PM||||To clarify my original reference to the magazine article:<P>"Pulp Friction"<BR>A Plain-Talk Primer on Your Car's Braking System, <BR>Grassroots Motorsports, February 2001, pages 72-81.<P>The author is James Walker, Jr., who is an anti-lock braking systems engineer with Robert Bosch Corp, and also races a Saturn in SCCA ITA class. His race team is on the web at <A HREF="http://www.teamscr.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.teamscr.com/</A> <P>The GRMS article appears to be a condensed version of one of the tech articles from his team's home page.  <P>||||reg||1
Z||000045||SteveJ||02-21-2001||05:08 PM||||I beleive everbody who first drives a Fiero is scared by the brakes. Ya do get used to 'em but they really are marginal. . .and its clear that the manufacturers are a bit heavy handed with the proportionong valve. So if there is liability in all this it is with GM who saved pennies to put this car out. When I think of liability I think about that proportioning valve. Deliberatly they lame up the brakes. . . and they know they are reducing more than they should to aviod rear lockup. . .and they put those tiny rotors on the front. I know people don't like to call 'em Chevette brakes but they really are too small for the application and then they go heavy-handed with the proportioning valve so there is not much braking in the back. I had a Buick that went over 100,000 miles before the rears needed new brakes. I didn't mind because that thing stopped when required. The Fiero more coasts to a stop. I put new rotors and pads on last fall and it stops better but I have never seen it lock up, not ever. <BR>Ever think about an adapter to move the caliper outboard 3/4 inch or so. The current tiny pads don't sweep anywhere near the outside of the rotor. Cheap fix, and except the adapter- factory parts. <BR>Liability for this is GM all the way just like the Corvair and the fuel tanks on pickups.||||reg||1
Z||000046||theogre||02-21-2001||07:01 PM||||I've locked mine. more than a few times. On dry pavement even. 87SC.<P>To move the caliper that amount... You would need new brackets made. You might could shim the brakets if they are bolted on the spindles. (I can't rememeber at the moment.) You don't want to go right to the edge but you could get closer. I don't know how much that would help.<P>There are more articles you might want to read at motor.com and motorage.com These are trade publications. Motorage is searchable. motor has PDF's of most articles. (The Motor PDF's include the graphics. the HTML doesn't)||||reg||1
Z||000047||theogre||02-23-2001||07:36 PM||||IMPORTANT UPDATE.<P>Thanks to 87GTZ34 I was able to find the patent for the step bore MC at <A HREF="http://www.uspto.gov" TARGET=_blank>www.uspto.gov</A> <P>The Step bore MC works pretty much like Rock and 87GT said. It was nice to finally be able to see exactly how it works internally. It answers allot of questions.<P>What this means.... Droping the diameter of the MC main bore should not cause a pedal travel problem with the OE calipers. The next one smaller looks to be the 24x36. Now it's a matter of finding one that actually fits in place. <P>For those that want to read the patents....  <A HREF="http://164.195.100.11/netahtml/search-bool.html" TARGET=_blank>http://164.195.100.11/netahtml/search-bool.html</A> <P>Term 1. = quick take-up<BR>term 2. = general motors<BR>any year<P>leave all other boxes with the default.<P>this will return about 45 hits. The main one is 4,133,178. That one explains the whole thing. the more recent ones are variations of the "blow off" valve design.<P>Ignoring the effect of the prop valve the smaller MC will keep the bias closer to OE but give more power to both front and rear axles. Any adjustment to the prop valve setup should be minimal if any. The end result should be stronger braking from the OE hardware.<P>Now as soon as I can get my hands on a MC that fits and 2-3 days for testing..... <P>Why if fit hard.... Ideally we want a MC that is an exact external replacment for the OE one. The idea is to avoid making up brake lines or fussing with the mounting. I want this to be a drop in upgrade.||||reg||4
Z||000048||theogre||02-27-2001||08:24 PM||||It looks like the trial 24mm x 36mm MC will have to come from either an 88 Fiero or a S10 truck. All the car 24 x 36 I'm seeing are for cross feed brake systems and won't work. (Unlike Fiero, Cross feed has a front and a rear wheel on each circuit. There are 4 outlets on the MC and the MC has integral prop valves.)<P>I Think the S10 one is the same MC layout as the fiero but has a diferant tank on it. I won't know for sure untill I can compare them.||||reg||1
Z||000049||87ECO5||03-27-2001||02:49 PM||||<B>theogre</B> - have you had an opportunity to research this further?||||reg||5
Z||000050||webbee||03-27-2001||04:10 PM||||Theogre -  Man I really appreciate your work on this and other topics as well!<P>I remember a car review of Fieros when they first came out and they raved about the 4 wheel disks really doing the job. After I got mine I found the breaks to be marginal. I've rebuilt/replaced everything but the MC and still don't have what I'd call good breaks. So much for the hype. <P>From following this thread I realize that it's the system design. I don't know about other forum members, but I hope you figure out the fix! <P>BIG THANKS FROM ME!||||reg||1
Z||000051||theogre||03-27-2001||08:12 PM||||I have not done any testing of alternate MC configurations on a stock Fiero.  Most everything I have is in the Brake Upgrade article in body&chassis in my cave. <P>The MC test canditates would still be either an 88 Fiero or S-10 MC. (Must be a power brake truck.) Note that reworking the lines between the master cyl and the balance block may be required for proper operation. This is decribed in how to get more volume from the MC in the upgrade article.<B>You must fully understand how the Fiero MC works before attempting this.</B><P>At this time there is no further development planned in this area or any other. Because of medical issues, I won't be able to fool around with any thing but actual repairs before at least august. (Yes. Once again I'll miss the charlisle show.)||||reg||1
Z||000052||Tryxalon||03-27-2001||08:51 PM||||1) Ogre - I want to add my appreciation of your work and the unselfish way you share it!! <P>2) Fiero's have MARGINAL BRAKES?? Huh?? Are you just stomping on the brakes and praying or are you driving ahead, modulating brakes, balancing power to conditions?<P>Have you guys ever driven a 60's musclecar?? <P>I have to agree with the article that raved about 4 wheel discs!! A Fiero handles fantastically, stops on a dime and gives change compared to the other similarly priced and equipped cars of it's era!! Maybe it isn't a Ferrari - but it is most unlike the land-going-barges that Detroit put out in those years!! <P>But, each of us has his own opinion!! <BR>||||reg||6
Z||000053||Pilrulz||08-17-2001||05:35 PM||||okay, so what's the moral, what's the answer, what is the best thing to do, and will someone just spell it out in plain english?<P>                                   pil||||reg||12
Z||000054||theogre||08-17-2001||07:25 PM||||There is no plane english. That's the reason I took on this much research and writing.<P>The "verdict" is that the 4 wheel Grand Am upgrade will always be illegal in the U.S. and the front only version will have issues until a diferant rotor is found that can be made to fit consistantly and correctly. The GA one's "hat section" is too tall.<P>I did recently update the cave article to try to consolidate and clear up what the Grand Am problems are.<P>V8Archie and a few others are offering kits of various types. You would need to contact them for more info.||||reg||1
